{
  "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
  "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Andre Belanger",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "\"A verdict by 11 is no verdict at all\" is a line from the Court earlier this year that ended Louisiana's nonunanimous jury scheme. On paper, it restored the full breadth of the Sixth Amendment's jury trial right to Louisianans. But we need to place the effect of this ruling into perspective. This laudable ruling would only apply to cases then pending or recently adjudicated. It meant nothing to Mr. Edwards, who is serving a life sentence at Angola for a verdict that would be illegal everywhere else, as Louisiana is the only place that would jail you for natural life on a nonunanimous verdict."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First is we have amici have provided some statistics on the actual exonerations coming out of Louisiana. Of the 65 or so cases that they've identified, half of those cases were eligible for a nonunanimous verdict, and from that population of half, half of those, or one quarter of the 65, were actual exonerations of nonunanimous jury verdicts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Good morning, counsel. I'd like to start with your first argument, that Ramos did not announce a new rule. I -- I'm certainly sympathetic to that point of view. I believe the Court had, for well over 100 years, spoken about the unanimity requirement, as you know."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Murrill",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ELIZABETH MURRILL ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MURRILL",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ramos is unquestionably a new rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice THOMAS",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What's your view of what the term \"accuracy\" means? Does it mean scientifically accurate both in acquittal and convictions, or is it loading -- or a thumb on the scale one way or the other to prevent inaccurate convictions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Puerto Rico and Oregon",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Puerto Rico and Oregon both filed amicus briefs emphasizing the -- their -- their belief that this would have a -- a very significant impact in their states."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, and most of what my totally separate question is, what do you do about Brown versus Louisiana? It says that it's retroactive because you have --a six-man -- six-person jury has to be unanimous. It can't be 5 to 1. So, if a six-person -- person jury can't be 5 to 1 -- a 12-person can't be 10 to 2, and if the first was fundamental, why isn't the second?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "we would take the Promise of Justice Initiative's numbers at face value and think 1600 is an awful lot of new trials."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF CHRISTOPHER G. MICHEL FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MICHEL: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The rule announced by this Court in Ramos applies prospectively and to convictions on direct appeal, but it does not apply to final convictions on federal collateral review. That result follows from a straightforward application of Teague. The Ramos rule is new because whatever disputes might exist about the precedential weight of Apodaca in this Court, it was at least reasonable for lower courts to rely on it when petitioner's conviction became final in 2011. And the rule is not watershed because it is not essential to accuracy or a fair trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, very briefly, does the federal government have any light to shed on the statistics that we've been talking about?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel, would you just briefly discuss the term \"accuracy\" and what you think it means in this context."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Michel",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5807",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0548110b4daad2cbb30aa446fd27c62a3b68b97a042cf43bb38a14c693ade6a8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0548110b4daa-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the Court has not always spoken with one voice on that, but there are certainly a number of opinions in which \"accuracy\" I think is understood just to mean factual accuracy."
    },
    {
